SM(517,{"n":"Romans 9-10 Beginning","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Romans 9-10 Beginning.mp3","d":2616.8,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.6,"Okay, we'll go ahead and get started."],[23.52,"And uh when we get started here in just a moment, we're gonna be picking up uh near the end of uh St."],[35.76,"Paul's Epistle to the Romans, uh chapter nine, and and we're gonna be picking up in verse thirty."],[43.6,"Um so we just have this last little bit here in uh in chapter nine, which I probably should have gone over last time, but that's okay."],[54.72,"We've got a long time last time."],[56.8,"Um, and this does lead into what we're gonna get into in in uh chapter 10."],[59.6,"uh Um, so to get us caught up, we had a little bit of a transition in uh chapters eight and nine."],[73.96,"So I won't go all the way back to the beginning, but just to sort of reorient us in this section of the epistle that we're in."],[82.2,"Uh in uh chapter eight, we saw that uh St."],[87.32,"Paul was sort of directly addressing uh the theme of inheritance and us as as heirs, co-heirs with Christ."],[97.96,"Christ being uh the Son of God proper, and us uh becoming sons of God. through grace"],[106.24,"And we talked about what that means in relation to the sons of God as the angels in the Old Testament who served in God's counsel and how human beings are now placed into that role when they're glorified, and how that relates to our understanding of sainthood."],[129.68,"This is sort of the destiny and the promise for humanity for which humanity was created in the first place."],[138.92,"And now in chapter 9, Saint Paul has sort of moved on from there to talking about the whole issue surrounding what's happened with. what he's called Israel according to the flesh"],[155.88,"And this would be not just the people who the word that's translated as Jews in the New Testament is actually Judeans."],[168.84,"It means the people of Judea, the Roman province, very literally."],[174.09,"And technically that's even distinct from Galileans."],[184.33,"But is Judeans."],[186.09,"And so when Saint Paul talks about Israel, even Israel according to the flesh, that's a bigger category."],[193.85,"He's going back into the Old Testament"],[198.13,"And he's talking about, and he's talked about this in the context of the descendants of Abraham."],[203.65,"We saw he focused on Isaac and then Jacob and Esau at the beginning of chapter 9."],[209.33,"So this is going this is going further back than that."],[212.53,"Yes, now there's just this sort of Judea left."],[216.21,"Ten and a half tribes are gone completely at the fall of the northern kingdom of Israel to the Assyrians."],[227.57,"So he's talking about Israel as a whole."],[231.41,"And what do we say about God's promises, starting with to Abraham?"],[238.15,"Has God not kept his promises?"],[242.35,"And the term he's used, and this is going to be important as we go forward here, describe that as is whether God has been righteous or not."],[251.15,"Because God's righteousness is a matter in this context of him keeping his promises."],[257.07,"God makes a promise, he fulfills that promise, and so that shows that he is righteous, he is just."],[268.43,"Did that not happen?"],[269.95,"Because look, all of these people, the majority of the Judeans, who are around now, have not embraced Jesus as the Messiah."],[279.94,"They've turned their back on him."],[281.86,"And so they havenen't.'t, they aren't, as he began chapter 9, they ar on a path that leads to salvation"],[288.76,"Because they've cut themselves off from God by cutting themselves off from Christ."],[294.28,"And this is something that St."],[295.88,"Paul is sorrowful about."],[299.24,"So in what we read so far of chapter 9, he's been describing how that happened."],[306.92,"And he says, well, number one, this shouldn't be so surprising."],[312.44,"Because it was never everyone born descended from Abraham, who was a part of the covenant and who was a part of the path of salvation in the first place."],[325.58,"So he says, you've got Isaac and Ishmael who are born from Abraham."],[330.74,"And it's Isaac who's considered the firstborn and who's the heir."],[334.76,"The heir of the promise, the one who inherits the promises."],[337.56,"And then he turned to Jacob and Esau."],[341.05,"Even though Esau was technically older by a few seconds, they were twins, but even though Esau came out first by a few seconds, was older by a few seconds, it's Jacob who's considered the heir and the inheritor of the promises. Old Testament as"],[359.29,"Paul is not saying that Esau or Ishmael were cut off from the love of God, went to hell, were evil."],[369.39,"Any of those things."],[370.27,"Because the scriptures contradict that."],[373.77,"Ishmael received promises from God."],[376.65,"His mother did."],[377.69,"He didn't hear them directly, but they were passed on to him from Hagar, his mother."],[383.34,"And we talked about, we went through in a little bit of detail, the sort of historical road that Esau and his descendants traveled."],[390.8,"Which was one where they also received a portion of the inheritance, a portion of the land promised to Abraham."],[396.48,"But the point that St."],[397.84,"Paul has made is that Esau and his descendants received that inheritance as co-heirs with the firstborn."],[408.16,"It was through Esau's relationship with Jacob, his brother.her, that he became a co-initor"],[417.56,"It was from the Edomites, their descendants' brotherhood with Israel, that they received a share."],[425.64,"And so St."],[427.08,"Paul made this point by giving two quotes."],[430.2,"Remember, he quoted Genesis, where at the time of the birth of Jacob and Esau, it said, the older shall serve the younger."],[440.6,"And so the relationship is that relationship is described as service."],[446.73,"Esau becomes Jacob's servant because he's the firstborn, and so becomes a co-inheritor."],[454.09,"And then he gives another quote from the other end of the Old Testament scriptures from Malachi."],[458.17,"He could have quoted Obadiah, because that's all oracles gets the Edomites, too"],[462.85,"But he he quotes Malachi that says, Jacob, I have loved Esau, I have hated, which is that judgment comes upon the Edomites, comes upon Esau's descendants, because they turned on Judah."],[477.41,"When Judah their brother was falling, when the Babylonians fell, they took that opportunity to rob them, to seize their territory."],[485.73,"They turned on them."],[486.93,"And so because they turned on God's firstborn, they cut themselves off from the inheritance, and God ended up expelling them from their land."],[501.49,"And so he's using this as. an analogy for what's happened now"],[507.85,"In order to be a co-heir, in order to inherit the promises of God that started with Abraham and then came all the way down, you do that through your relationship to Christ."],[521.37,"You do that through serving Christ."],[525.77,"By serving Christ, you become a co-heir."],[532.49,"He shares that with those who serve him."],[538.45,"If you, like many of the Judeans did, reject him."],[544.71,"Reject God's firstborn, cut yourself off from him, you also cut yourself off from that inheritance."],[547.67,"The same way the Edomites ultimately did"],[555.07,"So that's the analogy he's made."],[558.43,"And he sort of concluded the section of chapter 9 that we read with a whole series of quotes from the Old Testament that talk about the fact that all of those promises toward Israel, all of Israel's history, was aimed at producing this seed."],[577.99,"The one who would finally inherit."],[582.07,"And so it was bringing forth Christ that was the reason for all these things that happened in the Old Testament."],[589.99,"And it's also important to remember because it's going to come up again here in a minute."],[595.43,"He talked about, he spent some time addressing what he called vessels of wrath."],[600.23,"He appealed to the imagery of Jeremiah that talked about God as like a potter with a potter's wheel, and he makes a nation, and one of them gets used to drink water out of, and another one gets used as a chamber pot."],[614.41,"One's for honorably once for dishonorable use."],[617.61,"And he talked about Pharaoh in the Old Testament having been raised up and made this glorious king on the earth so that God could defeat him and humiliate him, you know, in the Exodus."],[630.37,"Um, and this is St."],[632.01,"Paul responding to the question: you know, well, is God just playing games?"],[639.25,"Why did God bring 12 tribes out of Egypt if ten and a half of them were just gonna get wiped out?"],[646.85,"And he knew it."],[649.11,"If he knew that the vast majority of people in the old covenant were not going to be faithful and were never going to inherit these promises, why did he bother to bring them out and do all this?"],[658.17,"Why bother raising Pharaoh up if you're just gonna knock him back down?"],[663.46,"And so then in the present time for Saint Paul, why is there this Jewish nation if only this tiny little remnant was actually going to accept the Messiah when he came?"],[676.71,"Why did these people even live?"],[678.39,"Why did these"],[680.6,"And uh it is in response to that question that Saint Paul has said, and he hasn't elaborated on it yet, he's going to, that's why I'm bringing it up again."],[690.52,"Has said that there were in each of those cases there were reasons."],[695.16,"And he talked about the reason in regard to Pharaoh."],[698.78,"Because through the Exodus and through the defeat of Pharaoh and the other gods of Egypt, God's name was made known."],[705.5,"Yahweh's name was made known to the whole world."],[708.96,"When the spies get to Jericho and or with Rahab, Rahab says, Oh, yeah, we heard about you guys and your God and what you did to Egypt."],[716.16,"Because they were the most powerful nation in the world at the time."],[719.36,"So there was a purpose there with Pharaoh."],[722.81,"And could Pharaoh really complain about all the blessings he received?"],[728.81,"Why'd you give me all of these incredible blessings if you were just going to take them away?"],[734.67,"He wasn't entitled to them in the first place."],[741.07,"Paul hasn't defined what it is yet, but he has suggested already that there is a reason why those ten and a half tribes, for example, that we're just going to fall away."],[753.19,"We're brought out of Egypt."],[755.07,"And there is a reason why now, those other Judeans are out there, even though they haven't yet accepted Jesus as the Messiah."],[765.09,"So he's kind of left that hanging"],[769.85,"And we're gonna get he's gonna explore that more as we go forward."],[772.89,"So just to set that up again."],[774.67,"He said, There is a reason, just like there was a reason with Pharaoh, there is a reason for this."],[782.75,"And so that that's one side."],[784.59,"The flip side then are those quotes that he gave just before we ended, where he talks about, for example, if it wasn't for the seed, that singular seed of Christ, we would have been like Sodom."],[795.23,"Or right, God would have wiped them out uh in his wrath a long time ago, if it was based on their obedience or disobedience purely."],[805.97,"And we see that again and again in the Old Testament."],[808.05,"St. Paul isn't just coming up here, not only in that quote that he uses, but over and over again, for example, when Judah is in sin, it's always, well, for the sake of my servant David, I will I will preserve a remnant."],[822.9,"And of course, David is the first anointed king who's the paradigm of the Messiah who's coming."],[828.92,"So Saint Paul is picking up on all this through what we call the Old Testament, through the Hebrew Scriptures."],[834.94,"And it's now making this argument."],[838.16,"To to Jews and Gentiles both."],[842.8,"Um that that, you know, this this was part of God's working all along."],[850.08,"Okay So unless we have any questions or comments or hurled insults or limericks or anything else, we'll go ahead and get started here in uh chapter 9, verse 30."],[870.12,"What shall we say then?"],[871.96,"The Gentiles who did not pursue righteousness have attained to righteousness, even the righteousness of faith, but Israel pursuing the law of righteousness has not attained to the law of righteousness."],[885.88,"So he's saying, it looks like."],[888.3,"These Gentiles out here who have now heard the gospel, who have embraced Jesus as the Messiah, as the savior of the world, they've now received righteousness."],[897.55,"Now remember, we talked about when righteousness is talked about in terms of people, what that means"],[905.51,"It means that on the day of judgment, you're on the right hand rather than the left hand."],[911.27,"You're a sheep, not a goat."],[913.48,"So these Gentiles weren't thinking about the day of the Lord."],[919.02,"The day of judgment."],[920.3,"They had no idea that was even a thing."],[922.75,"And yet they somehow now, through through their faithfulness to Christ, have received that status."],[931.34,"So when the day comes when Christ returns, they're going to be on the right hand, they're going to inherit."],[937.34,"Paul says, well, on one hand, that's happening"],[937.64,"And St. And on the other hand, these people who had received the Torah."],[944.7,"These Jewish people who were very concerned about the day of the Lord, the day of judgment."],[950.32,"And who have been trying to, at least some of them trying to keep the Torah."],[955.54,"Trying to write dear to God, somehow they've ended up on the wrong side of the equation."],[962.14,"Well, how do you explain that?"],[964.48,"How do you explain that?"],[967.36,"He says, why?"],[969.04,"Because they did not seek it by faith, but as it were by the works of the law."],[977.88,"For they stumbled at that stumbling stone, as it is written, Behold, I lay in Zion a stumbling stone and rock of offense, and whoever believes on him will not be put to shame."],[993.64,"So that's a that's a uh quote from Isaiah again."],[999.72,"So first he says, why?"],[1004.01,"How did this happen?"],[1005.77,"Because they, the they being the Judean people who were seeking it."],[1013.15,"Did not seek it by faith, but as it were by the works of the law."],[1018.43,"ell, W what does that mean?"],[1020.95,"I'm not asking you to tell me."],[1024.23,"Okay. Okay So, how are how were they trying to establish their status as an heir of the promises?"],[1036.81,"Well, by following all the laws as exactly."],[1041.51,"By keeping the Torah."],[1042.47,"They're trying to establish it themselves."],[1045.41,"By keeping not just the commandments that are actually in the Torah, but all of the interpretations and all of the right and all of this besides."],[1058.05,"Remember, we talked about how this has been skewed because of our Protestant friends, what faith is."],[1066.29,"Faith is not just, I check the truefalse box."],[1069.09,"Faith implies obedience"],[1069.79,"Jesus is the Son of God, true."],[1071.89,"Faith implies faithfulness."],[1075.89,"And this is in the context, remember, we're in Romans 9 still, St."],[1080.45,"Paul's been talking about service."],[1082.67,"Rather than by trying to be faithful to their God."],[1087.25,"Rather than trying to be faithful, on their side to the promises."],[1092.41,"And serve God."],[1094.17,"And therefore receive the gift of being made an heir."],[1099.09,"They've been trying to do what they've worked out will entitle them to be an heir."],[1107.83,"So if I keep all this perfectly, God is force."],[1109.67,"Then, yes Then God's gonna come and do and do this for me."],[1118.91,"Then God's gonna come and do this for me."],[1121.71,"And we've talked before when we're going through the Gospels and even in Acts a little bit, uh, about the view of the Pharisees."],[1129.81,"The Pharisees were doing the math, and they said, Well, look, the reason we got sent into exile, the reason everything went south and we lost the kingdom, is because we were disobedient."],[1141.1,"They were right about that."],[1142.16,"That's pretty clear when you read the prophets."],[1143.98,"We did keep the Torah, we didn't do what we were supposed to be doing, we were disobedient to God."],[1148.38,"So, we're still in this situation, even though we're back living in Palestine."],[1152.51,"These promises haven't really been fulfilled yet"],[1153.51,"We're still in this situation where God hasn't returned to the temple."],[1159.01,"And so the way to get God to keep his promises, the way to get God to send the Messiah, is for all of us to obey it perfectly."],[1169.98,"This is why they they hated and labeled people as sinners."],[1184.46,"The Romans are here and oppressing us, not because the Romans are no good, but because we've got all these sinners here."],[1190.38,"And God is using them to judge us for their sins."],[1193.66,"And we're suffering too."],[1195.02,"This is why they get upset with Jesus for healing on the Saturn."],[1198.4,"And for hanging around with them."],[1200.62,"Why they're going to stone the woman to death for committing adultery."],[1204.24,"Because we've got to remove these sinners."],[1207.02,"And then God will send his Messiah in response to what we're doing."],[1213.43,"Because we've finally gotten it right."],[1219.03,"And so this is why the quote that St."],[1221.67,"Paul uses here is important."],[1225.83,"Because he's quoting Isaiah."],[1229.35,"And in this quote in Isaiah, if the rock, the stone that he's talking about, is placed by God."],[1236.77,"Meaning, God didn't send. the Messiah in response to them doing something"],[1243.45,"He didn't send the Messiah as a reward."],[1246.09,"He sent the Messiah in his timing."],[1249.29,"As St. Paul has already said, while we were still sinners, Christ loved us and died for us."],[1263.37,"Placed him in Zion."],[1264.83,"Jerusalem, Judea, in the midst of his people."],[1268.83,"And he's saying, and they've tripped over him."],[1273.79,"Because they weren't willing to accept him and who he was and what God was doing through him because they had the idea that he should have been doing something different."],[1288.19,"And it should have been related to what they were doing, which was different, and so they failed to understand that, and so they tripped."],[1299.11,"They sort of tripped over the rock, rather than that rock serving as a foundation for them."],[1307.4,"Okay, and they were set up for that by the fact that they were approaching the whole thing in the wrong way in the first place."],[1315.8,"What would be, can you describe a little bit?"],[1319.36,"What would be the they did not seek it by faith."],[1324.96,"What would it be like if they had sought it by faith?"],[1325.72,"they What would be doing?"],[1329.2,"Well, those those are the people who we see who accepted Jesus as the Messiah."],[1333.44,"That's one of the major themes in St."],[1334.88,"John's Gospel, for example, is that the people who have already been faithfully serving God when they recognize Jesus and who he is when they encounter him."],[1349.58,"So they're listening."],[1353.03,"They're actively, they're actively following God."],[1359.99,"And this is a theme in St."],[1361.43,"Luke's Gospel, too."],[1362.31,"I mean, it's all through the Gospels in general."],[1364.07,"But for example, you know, St."],[1366.15,"Simeon and Saint Anna."],[1368.65,"He's a baby."],[1370.78,"How many babies were probably brought to the temple every day to be dedicated?"],[1375.66,"You know, from all over Judea and Galilee."],[1378.3,"Yet they see this baby and they know it's the Messiah."],[1382.4,"He wasn't glowing."],[1385.04,"He didn't. He just looked like, but they were attuned."],[1389.75,"They were seeking the kingdom of God."],[1393.73,"They were faithfully following God."],[1395.73,"And so when they saw Jesus, even as a baby, as an infant, they knew who he was."],[1402.37,"They recognized who he was."],[1406.13,"Yeah, so this is what this is what he's talking about."],[1411.85,"And he includes himself in this."],[1415.13,"In his former life as a Pharisee."],[1418.51,"That he had these ideas, he had these traditions."],[1422.56,"That that and these traditions were the right way to live."],[1426.64,"He was doing it right."],[1429.2,"And because Jesus didn't fit into his paradigm of who the Messiah was going to be and what he was going to do, he rejected him."],[1438.74,"And so thoroughly rejected him, he was out murdering Christians."],[1443.22,"He was out murdering his followers."],[1445.06,"So he's including himself in this."],[1447.38,"He knows exactly what that's like."],[1449.3,"But this is what Jesus was trying to get across to them all the time, too."],[1452.82,"When he talked to them about them having their traditions that nullified the Torah."],[1457.03,"The problem wasn't the Torah."],[1458.87,"The problem is the way they were using it, the way they were interpreting it, and what they were adding to it."],[1472.71,"He says to them, you know, you tithe of mint and dill and cumin."],[1476.69,"Meaning you're so fastidious about tithing."],[1479.73,"You go out there and take 10% of your herb garden."],[1483.97,"Little snippers, and you're out there, and you put them in little bags."],[1487.25,"And you know, and you bring those to the temple because the tithe."],[1491.83,"Gotta give 10%, gotta give the first fruits."],[1494.53,"He says, but you neglect the deeper things of the law."],[1497.67,"Mercy. truth, justice, compassion"],[1501.87,"And Jesus doesn't say those things are important and tithing isn't."],[1506.51,"He says, these things you should have done while not neglecting the others either."],[1513.87,"So the problem isn't with the Torah itself."],[1516.83,"It isn't even with works of the law or works of the Torah."],[1520.67,"It's when you try to use them for something for which they were never intended."],[1526.27,"That's another thing, remember that St."],[1527.95,"Paul has talked about already in Romans is what the original purpose of the Torah was."],[1535.01,"The purpose of the Torah is not to make people sons of God."],[1540.83,"It can't do that."],[1543.73,"The purpose of the Torah always was, as this sort of, like we talked about, sin management system."],[1551.43,"To manage the sin of the people."],[1554.0,"To allow God to stay in their midst, to allow them to draw close to God and to sort of manage the problems of sin and evil and corruption and death in the world until the Messiah came to deal with death and sin once and for all."],[1570.32,"And so that's why that's why there's now a shift in the way the Torah works."],[1576.88,"It's not done away with, but there's a shift in how it works because it's not performing that function anymore."],[1586.02,"So we don't need to keep doing sin offerings and sacrificing goats at the Day of Atonement."],[1592.68,"And killing all the animals."],[1594.92,"Because Christ has taken care of the sin and the guilt, and that those were taken care of, and the stain of sin left behind that the Day of Atonement took care of."],[1604.2,"Christ is taking care of that once and for all."],[1607.1,"So that isn't all done away with."],[1608.78,"We now have the Eucharist."],[1610.48,"That is the fulfillment of all of those offerings, and the grain offerings, and the drink offerings, and the thank offerings."],[1616.64,"All of that finds its fulfillment in the Eucharist, which continues."],[1621.2,"So it's not done away with, it's fulfilled and elevated to a new level."],[1628.92,"And so he's saying that they're trying to use the Torah for something for which it was never intended."],[1636.12,"And what they're trying to use it for is to establish their own righteousness."],[1641.01,"To establish their own status, at the right hand of God."],[1647.05,"Through their own efforts."],[1650.87,"By keeping by keeping the Torah as they have interpreted it perfectly."],[1655.77,"And then they're there, and everyone else's is over there."],[1659.85,"It's interesting that the prophets who are telling the people, you know, God's going to punish you for not following the law, always talk about things like uh taking care of widows and orphans and being compassionate and merciful and things like that."],[1679.65,"They never talk about you ate pork."],[1682.45,"Yeah. Yeah. Stop with the pork eating."],[1686.61,"Yeah. Yeah, that's never their their focus."],[1692.13,"Yeah. It was the it was the deeper things."],[1694.93,"Yeah. You know, they're probably eating pork too."],[1698.13,"Uh-huh. But, you know, if you're sacrificing to Baal and uh stealing the land of orphans and widows and eating pork."],[1709.85,"Two of those are more important than the other."],[1713.25,"Are more serious."],[1714.54,"We can deal with the pork eating.ing later once we've dealt with the other massive, the massive th"],[1721.3,"Yeah, and that's that's not a that's not just a subjective judgment of what's a serious sin and what's not as serious a sin."],[1730.66,"Remember when we talked about Acts 15 and the specific rules that were put on the Gentiles."],[1737.48,"The portions of the Torah that applied to the Gentiles, we went back and we talked about in Leviticus 17 through 21, the Holiness Code."],[1748.06,"That there were specific sins."],[1750.78,"There were a whole bunch of things that the Israelites were commanded to do, like not eat pork."],[1756.32,"Like all the food laws, all those things."],[1758.56,"There were only certain commandments that applied to everyone who lived in the land."],[1766.01,"Jew, Gentile, believing, unbelieving."],[1769.29,"It didn't matter."],[1770.01,"Anyone who lived in the land had to follow these."],[1772.81,"Because these sins actually corrupted and tainted the land."],[1778.65,"It uses the language of the land will vomit you out to get rid of you."],[1784.82,"If you do these things."],[1786.1,"And so no one could be allowed to do these things."],[1789.86,"And so those are the sins, for the most part, that the prophets talk about."],[1796.72,"These things are unacceptable to anyone."],[1805.28,"And not to make too much of a social comment, but the two big ones, of course, are idolatry and sexual immorality, both of which are deeply protected freedoms in our country."],[1822.48,"Make of that what you will."],[1833.78,"That is of greater consequence."],[1835.3,"And that that language isn't just used in the Old Testament and isn't just reflected in Acts 15."],[1840.42,"But if you read the pieces we have of uh St."],[1844.98,"Justinian's Nomo Canon, that's the basis for the whole Byzantine idea of the Nomo Canon that you which is canon law and civil law brought together."],[1855.38,"The pieces of canon law, the pieces of church law that apply to everyone that becomes civil law, it uses the language of they're the sins that corrupt the ground."],[1867.36,"Those can't be allowed at all."],[1870.56,"At all. Come here from the court of the caliph and he doesn't fast on Wednesday, okay."],[1880.52,"We can live with that."],[1881.96,"Okay. But he can't commit acts of idolatry or sexual immorality while he's here."],[1889.64,"Those are a different, those are a different category."],[1892.68,"But so that understanding carries through centuries and centuries into Christianity."],[1899.24,"Um that there's that different class."],[1903.32,"That different class of sins."],[1908.04,"St. Paul is focusing here on the idea that, rather than trying to faithfully follow God and being rewarded with the gift of his inheritance."],[1919.99,"They were sort of approaching it like a contract."],[1923.61,"Here's the requirements on my end."],[1927.11,"And they would say God is righteous, so he's going to live up to the the uh his side of bargain, as long as I keep mine."],[1941.63,"And so that's what creates the whole dilemma that St."],[1944.03,"Paul's talking about."],[1945.01,"A Pharisee who didn't accept Jesus as the Messiah could stand in front of St."],[1948.61,"Paul and say, hey, I'm keeping the Torah perfectly."],[1953.33,"I don't think this guy's the Messiah."],[1958.66,"And if he is the Messiah, then he should have come here to save me."],[1962.26,"Because I'm keeping the Torah perfectly."],[1966.18,"And so this is St."],[1967.22,"Paul's answer to that."],[1970.18,"That's his answer to that."],[1971.62,"And then implied is the flip side that the reason these Gentiles have actually become heirs. now is because they're faithfully trying to follow God in Jesus Christ"],[1985.74,"Even though they're not keeping all the details of the they're not getting circumcised."],[1990.22,"They're not doing all of these, all of these other things."],[2009.9,"For I bear them witness that they have a zeal for God, but not according to knowledge."],[2015.34,"Okay So this is how, again, St."],[2019.42,"Paul's including himself in this."],[2022.23,"So he says, look they have all this zeal."],[2026.21,"And zeal is not just excitement or enthusiasm."],[2031.01,"Zeal is sort of a technical term."],[2033.09,"Right as well."],[2033.97,"There's zealots. Zealots are a thing at this time in history."],[2039.49,"The tradition of zealotry goes back to probably the prime motivator of it is Phineas in the book of Numbers."],[2052.05,"And uh there was this episode, uh this really unfortunate episode when the Israelites were nearing, entering into the land."],[2061.01,"They came to this place called Peor, and there was a high place there where they worshipped. Baal"],[2068.01,"Baal of Peor, and uh Baal of Peor had some priestesses, is the polite way to say it."],[2076.73,"Uh, the more accurate way would be what we would call temple prostitutes, uh, who took care of that high place, and they came to some of the men of Israel and enticed them with various enticements, um, which you can imagine, which got them to not only engage with them sexually, but also do that in a ritual context related to Baal worship."],[2107.61,"Okay. This is obviously very bad."],[2111.49,"And when Moses and others and the other elders of the people did not immediately take action about this, God sent a plague on the camp to try to indicate: hey, something's wrong."],[2127.67,"You need to take care of this."],[2131.03,"Despite that, there's then this incident where an Israelite man and one of these women are so brazen that they walk past Moses and Aaron and the elders who are standing in front of the tent of meeting where they meet with God."],[2145.99,"Walk right past them together and go into a tent and uh have sexual relations. where people knew that was happening in the tent right in front of them"],[2161.63,"And so Phineas uh picked up a spear, walked into the tent, and put the spear through both of them and killed them."],[2173.23,"And as soon as he did that, the plague lifted."],[2179.31,"So this story, and because of that, because he was the only one who did anything about it, Phineas uh was given uh a promised priesthood by God."],[2194.19,"Um, interestingly, Phineas has uh an Egyptian name, Pinhas."],[2199.39,"So technically he, wasn't a child of Abraham."],[2200.67,"He had become part of Israel the other way when they had left Egypt"],[2208.31,"But so the way he was sort of held up as an example of faith and faithfulness started this tradition of zealotry."],[2219.68,"That it's not enough for a person to themselves not sin."],[2230.32,"If they see sin going on in the presence of God, they need to do something about it."],[2238.24,"They need to act, including violent action."],[2243.6,"And the zealots of of this time, Simon the Zealot, remember, was one of Jesus' disciples, one the twelve."],[2253.0,"It's likely Judas Iscariot that the Iscariot is related to him being one of the Sicarii, who are also a zealot faction, who are basically assassins, murdered a lot of Romans."],[2266.44,"They were there to, again, take action."],[2270.84,"While the Pharisees were waiting for God to come and act."],[2275.98,"When we get this all right, God will act."],[2278.56,"The zealots were taking this into their own hands."],[2280.8,"No, we need to rise up and get rid of these Romans."],[2283.78,"These pagans. That's what God wants us to do."],[2286.02,"They're pagan idolaters."],[2287.42,"They're oppressing his people."],[2288.5,"We need to be like the judges in the book of Judges"],[2288.94,"We need to be like Phineas."],[2293.42,"We need to take these take these guys out through violence if necessary."],[2299.39,"So when Saint Paul appeals to zeal."],[2304.71,"He's appealing to this tradition, which is a tradition he was located in."],[2308.07,"Remember, he's going out murdering Christians for being heretic."],[2314.31,"So he was he was in there."],[2315.91,"He was out there taking, he wasn't just sitting around going, oh, these no good heretic Christians."],[2320.55,"He was out there going from city to city with orders from the high priest, having people imprisoned, having people killed."],[2326.68,"Saint Stephen, stoned to death."],[2329.46,"So he's saying, look, there's a lot of zeal out there."],[2332.88,"They're zealous. They're willing to take action."],[2335.87,"But it's not according to knowledge."],[2338.35,"They don't understand what they're doing."],[2340.51,"And therefore, relatedly, St."],[2342.35,"Paul is saying that guy Saul of Tarsus didn't understand what he was doing."],[2347.33,"All the zeal of the world, but I didn't have the knowledge to know what I was doing."],[2352.77,"It was misdirected."],[2354.87,"Completely misdirected. For they being ignorant of God's righteousness and seeking to establish their own righteousness, have not submitted to the righteousness of God."],[2371.67,"So they don't know the righteousness of God, which is who?"],[2373.75,"He's already said that in chapter 3"],[2376.35,"Christ. Christ is the righteousness of God."],[2381.13,"So not understanding that Jesus is the Messiah, not understanding what God is doing in Jesus as the Messiah."],[2391.05,"Because they don't understand that, they're trying to become righteous through this other way."],[2399.13,"This other way."],[2400.89,"And because they're trying to do it this other way, they've missed out on what God is doing."],[2404.33,"missed out on what God is doing. They"],[2410.49,"And he uses this language of submitting themselves."],[2415.05,"Submitting themselves. Remember that the older shall serve the younger."],[2418.33,"This idea of you have to serve the heir"],[2423.89,"They won't submit."],[2426.69,"God has to do things the way they think he should do things."],[2431.33,"And so they won't accept this is the Messiah who God has sent."],[2435.25,"This is what he was doing."],[2436.21,"This is how he's doing it."],[2437.81,"I'm going to submit myself to that."],[2441.81,"They refuse. And so they've missed out."],[2448.77,"He says, for Christ is the end of the law for righteousness to everyone who believes."],[2454.13,"The word end there is not end like it's over now."],[2459.99,"Christ came, so chucked the law out in the trash can."],[2462.55,"End is in purpose"],[2462.91,"It's end, it's the Greek word telos."],[2469.71,"End is in purpose."],[2472.91,"Christ is the purpose for which the Torah was given."],[2475.47,"It's what the Torah was pointing to."],[2478.03,"It wasn't pointing to itself."],[2481.63,"The Torah was not just about how great the Torah is."],[2485.31,"The Torah had an aim."],[2487.2,"And that was that was Christ."],[2489.6,"And so it's in Christ that we find God's righteousness."],[2492.48,"It's in Christ that God keeps his promises."],[2495.28,"It's in Jesus Christ."],[2498.24,"That's where God keeps his promises."],[2501.08,"Well, the efforts of the Pharisees to keep all the laws exactly, that's actually the stumbling block, then, isn't it?"],[2515.4,"That's what keeps them from keeps them from having their eyes, their eyes are so fixed on this that they can't see."],[2525.16,"They're pursuing the law as an end in itself."],[2527.88,"Yeah. Pursuing the Torah as an end in itself."],[2531.24,"This is the righteousness of God."],[2533.0,"This is where God keeps his promises, is the Torah and keeping it perfectly."],[2537.0,"The Gentiles who don't have that focus, in a sense, have it easier."],[2543.96,"They can see it more readily."],[2546.74,"They don't have that competition."],[2548.26,"Uh-huh. Yeah. Now they have other issues."],[2550.9,"es Y. Yeah"],[2551.42,"Yeah. But they don't have that big stumbling block."],[2558.78,"All right. And so Christ is the place where God keeps his promises to everyone who is faithful."],[2565.28,"Where we talked about faith and believe, this is the same word in Greek."],[2569.5,"To all the faithful."],[2571.5,"And by all he means Jews, Gentiles, doesn't matter."],[2576.54,"If you're faithful to Christ, if you submit to Christ, the righteousness of God, then that's where you receive God's promises."],[2585.58,"Because He was the purpose of the law in the first place."],[2589.74,"It wasn't an end in itself."],[2591.42,"That's not where the promises were kept."],[2594.46,"Listen next time. as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the whole counsel of God"],[2601.78,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2607.14,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":21.6,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: Romans 8-9 so far","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up <b>Romans 9:30</b> - last bit of ch. 9, leads into <b>ch. 10</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ch. 8</b>: theme of <b>inheritance</b> - <b>heirs, co-heirs with Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ = Son of God proper; we become <b>sons of God by grace</b></li><li class=\"d1\">tied to <b>sons of God = angels</b> in God's counsel; humans placed in that role when <b>glorified</b> → <b>sainthood</b></li><li class=\"d1\">= destiny humanity was <b>created for</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ch. 9</b>: moves to <b>Israel according to the flesh</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"Jews\" in NT = actually <b>Judeans</b>, people of the <b>Roman province</b>; technically distinct from <b>Galileans</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Israel is a <b>bigger category</b>; <b>ten and a half tribes</b> gone at fall of the north to <b>Assyria</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Question: has God <b>not kept his promises</b> - starting with <b>Abraham</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>righteousness</b> = God <b>keeping his promises</b> (key going forward)</li><li class=\"d0\">Majority of Judeans haven't embraced <b>Jesus</b> → cut themselves off from God by cutting off from Christ - Paul is <b>sorrowful</b></li></ul>"},{"t":306.92,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: how it happened","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not surprising - <b>never everyone descended from Abraham</b> was in the covenant</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Isaac vs Ishmael</b>: Isaac = firstborn, <b>heir of the promise</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Ishmael <b>received promises</b> via his mother <b>Hagar</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Jacob vs Esau</b> (twins, a few seconds apart): Jacob = heir</li><li class=\"d1\">Esau <b>not cut off from God's love</b>, not evil - Scripture contradicts it</li><li class=\"d1\">Esau's line got <b>part of the land</b>, as <b>co-heirs with the firstborn</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Two quotes</li><li class=\"d1\">Genesis: \"<b>older shall serve the younger</b>\" - relationship = <b>service</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Malachi</b>: \"Jacob I loved, Esau I hated\" (could have used <b>Obadiah</b>) - Edomites <b>turned on Judah</b> when <b>Babylon</b> fell</li><li class=\"d0\">Analogy: to be <b>co-heir</b> you <b>serve Christ</b>; <b>reject God's firstborn</b> → cut off, like <b>Edom</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Series of OT quotes: all of Israel's history <b>aimed at producing the seed</b></li></ul>"},{"t":589.99,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: vessels of wrath and the purpose in it","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Vessels of wrath</b>: <b>Jeremiah</b> imagery of <b>potter</b> - one for water, one a <b>chamber pot</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Pharaoh</b> raised up so God could <b>defeat and humiliate</b> him in the <b>Exodus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul answering: is God <b>playing games</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">why bring out <b>12 tribes</b> if <b>10.5</b> wiped out?</li><li class=\"d1\">why raise Pharaoh just to knock him down?</li><li class=\"d1\">why does a Jewish nation exist if only a <b>tiny remnant</b> accepts the Messiah?</li></ul>"},{"t":680.6,"lv":3,"h":"Paul's answer: there were reasons","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul: <b>there were reasons</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Pharaoh: God's <b>name made known</b> to the whole world - <b>Rahab</b> at Jericho</li><li class=\"d1\">Pharaoh not <b>entitled</b> to his blessings in the first place</li><li class=\"d0\">Reason for the ten and a half tribes, and for Judeans not yet accepting - <b>left hanging</b>; Paul <b>explores it later</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Flip side: but for the <b>singular seed</b>, we'd be like <b>Sodom</b></li><li class=\"d1\">OT again and again: \"for the sake of <b>my servant David</b>, I'll preserve a remnant\"</li><li class=\"d1\">David = <b>first anointed king</b>, paradigm of the <b>Messiah</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Argument to <b>Jews and Gentiles</b> both: <b>part of God's working all along</b></li></ul>"},{"t":850.08,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 9:30-33 - Gentiles attain righteousness by faith","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Questions/comments/\"<b>limericks</b>\" invited - then v.30</li><li class=\"d0\">v.30-31: <b>Gentiles</b> who didn't pursue righteousness <b>attained</b> it - <b>righteousness of faith</b>; <b>Israel</b> pursuing the law <b>has not</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Righteousness</b> of people = on <b>day of judgment</b> you're on the <b>right hand</b>, a <b>sheep not a goat</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Gentiles weren't thinking about the <b>day of the Lord</b> - \"no idea that was even a thing\"</li><li class=\"d1\">through <b>faithfulness to Christ</b> they have that status → on the right hand when Christ returns</li><li class=\"d0\">Jews who received the <b>Torah</b>, concerned about <b>day of judgment</b>, trying to <b>keep the Torah</b> → on the <b>wrong side</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"How do you explain that?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">v.32: \"did not seek it <b>by faith</b>, but <b>as it were by works of the law</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>stumbled at that stumbling stone</b>\" - quote from <b>Isaiah</b>: \"I lay in Zion a <b>stumbling stone and rock of offense</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Whoever believes on him will <b>not be put to shame</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">promise: believers <b>not shamed</b> on the day of judgment</li></ul>"},{"t":1024.23,"lv":3,"h":"Works of the law and what faith means","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">How were they trying to establish their status as <b>heirs</b>? <b>Keeping the Torah exactly</b>, <b>establishing it themselves</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just the Torah's commands but <b>all the interpretations</b> and more</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Protestants</b> skewed what <b>faith</b> is</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"check the <b>true/false box</b>: Jesus is the Son of God\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Jesus is the Son of God - true; but <b>faith implies faithfulness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">in context of <b>Romans 9</b>, faith = <b>serving</b> God and receiving the <b>gift</b> of being made heir</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>faith implies obedience, faithfulness</b>; Paul has been talking about <b>service</b></li><li class=\"d0\">They worked out what would <b>entitle</b> them to be heir: \"keep all this perfectly → God is <b>forced</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1121.71,"lv":3,"h":"The Pharisees did the math","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Pharisees</b> did the math</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>exile</b> and lost kingdom = our <b>disobedience</b> (right about that - prophets)</li><li class=\"d1\">back in Palestine but promises <b>not fulfilled</b>; God <b>hasn't returned to the temple</b></li><li class=\"d1\">so: <b>all obey perfectly</b> → God sends the <b>Messiah</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Why they <b>hated and labeled people sinners</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Romans</b> oppress us because of the <b>sinners</b>; God using them to judge</li><li class=\"d1\">why upset at Jesus <b>healing on the Sabbath</b>, <b>eating with sinners</b></li><li class=\"d1\">why stone the <b>woman caught in adultery</b> - remove sinners, then Messiah comes</li></ul>"},{"t":1219.03,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 9:33 - The stone God placed","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Isaiah quote: the stone is <b>placed by God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God <b>didn't send the Messiah in response</b> to what they did, <b>not as a reward</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sent in <b>his timing</b>: \"while we were still sinners, <b>Christ loved us and died for us</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Placed in <b>Zion</b>, in the midst of his people - \"they've <b>tripped over him</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">they had an idea he <b>should have been doing something different</b>, related to <b>their</b> efforts</li><li class=\"d1\">tripped over the rock instead of it being their <b>foundation</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Set up for it: they approached the whole thing <b>the wrong way</b> from the start</li><li class=\"d0\">Not a reward for <b>works</b>: God's timing, God's initiative</li></ul>"},{"t":1315.8,"lv":3,"h":"Seeking it by faith","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: what would seeking it <b>by faith</b> look like?</li><li class=\"d1\">those who <b>accepted Jesus</b> - theme in <b>St. John's Gospel</b>: people <b>already faithfully serving God</b> recognize him</li><li class=\"d1\">also <b>Luke</b>: <b>Simeon and Anna</b> - baby among many brought to the temple daily; \"he wasn't <b>glowing</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">they were <b>attuned</b>, seeking the kingdom → recognized him</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>faithfully following God</b>, so even as an <b>infant</b> they knew who he was</li><li class=\"d1\">those who accepted Jesus = people <b>already actively following God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul includes himself: <b>former Pharisee</b>, Jesus didn't fit his paradigm → <b>murdering Christians</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he knows <b>exactly what that's like</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same thing Jesus tried to get across: <b>traditions</b> vs Torah</li></ul>"},{"t":1449.3,"lv":2,"h":"Jesus on traditions that nullified the Torah","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Jesus' message: <b>traditions that nullified the Torah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">problem = <b>how they used it, interpreted it, added to it</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"You tithe <b>mint, dill, cumin</b>\" - <b>snippers, little bags</b> to the temple</li><li class=\"d1\">\"but neglect <b>mercy, truth, justice, compassion</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">he doesn't say tithing is unimportant - \"<b>while not neglecting the others</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1526.27,"lv":3,"h":"The Torah's real purpose","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Problem isn't the Torah or <b>works of the law</b> - it's using them for what they were <b>never intended</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Purpose of Torah <b>not to make people sons of God</b> - can't</li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>sin management system</b>: God stays in their midst, manage <b>sin, evil, corruption, death</b> until the <b>Messiah</b> deals with them <b>once and for all</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So a <b>shift</b> in how it works - <b>not done away with</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no more <b>sin offerings</b>, <b>Day of Atonement</b>, goats - <b>Christ</b> took care of sin, guilt, stain</li><li class=\"d1\">now the <b>Eucharist</b> = fulfillment of <b>grain, drink, thank offerings</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>fulfilled and elevated</b> to a new level\"</li><li class=\"d0\">They use it to <b>establish their own righteousness</b>, status at <b>God's right hand</b>, through <b>own efforts</b>, \"and everyone else's is over there\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1659.85,"lv":3,"h":"The prophets and the weightier sins","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Prophets</b> warning of punishment: <b>widows and orphans, compassion, mercy</b> - never <b>pork</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>sacrificing to Baal</b>, stealing land of orphans and widows, eating pork - two are <b>more serious</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Q: not just <b>subjective</b> ranking</li><li class=\"d1\">only certain commandments applied to <b>all who lived in the land</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1730.66,"lv":3,"h":"Acts 15 and the Holiness Code","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Acts 15</b>: rules on <b>Gentiles</b> - from <b>Leviticus 17-21, Holiness Code</b></li><li class=\"d1\">many things only for <b>Israelites</b> (pork, <b>food laws</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">some applied to <b>everyone in the land</b> - Jew, Gentile, believing or not</li><li class=\"d1\">these sins <b>corrupted and tainted the land</b>: \"the land will <b>vomit you out</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Mostly the sins the prophets talk about</li><li class=\"d1\">two big ones: <b>idolatry</b> and <b>sexual immorality</b> - \"both deeply <b>protected freedoms</b> in our country. Make of that what you will\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Carries on: <b>Justinian's *Nomocanon</b>* = <b>canon law + civil law</b> together</li><li class=\"d1\">church law applying to <b>everyone</b> becomes civil law - \"sins that <b>corrupt the ground</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">guest from the <b>caliph's court</b> who doesn't <b>fast Wednesday</b> - can live with that; <b>idolatry or sexual immorality</b> - no</li><li class=\"d1\">different <b>class of sins</b>, carried through <b>centuries</b> of Christianity</li><li class=\"d1\">understanding continues into <b>Christianity</b>: that <b>different class</b> of sins</li></ul>"},{"t":1908.04,"lv":3,"h":"Contract thinking and the Pharisee's objection","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Instead of faithfully following and being <b>rewarded with inheritance</b>, they treated it like a <b>contract</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"here's my <b>requirements</b>; <b>God is righteous</b> so he'll live up to his side\"</li><li class=\"d0\">The dilemma: a <b>Pharisee</b> could say to Paul \"I'm keeping the Torah <b>perfectly</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I don't think this guy's the Messiah\" - and if he is, he should have come to <b>save me</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's answer; implied flip side: <b>Gentiles</b> are heirs because they <b>faithfully follow God in Jesus Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">though <b>not circumcised</b>, not doing other details</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: that's why the <b>Gentiles</b> are on the right side</li></ul>"},{"t":2009.9,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 10:1-3 - Zeal not according to knowledge","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.2: \"they have a <b>zeal for God</b>, but <b>not according to knowledge</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul <b>includes himself</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Zeal</b> = technical term - <b>zealots</b> at this time</li></ul>"},{"t":2039.49,"lv":3,"h":"Zeal and Phineas","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Origin: <b>Phineas</b> in <b>Numbers</b> - the incident at <b>Peor</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Baal of Peor</b>, high place, <b>priestesses</b> = <b>temple prostitutes</b></li><li class=\"d1\">enticed Israelite men into sex in a <b>ritual context of Baal worship</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Moses and elders didn't act → <b>plague</b> on the camp</li><li class=\"d1\">Israelite man + woman walk <b>past Moses, Aaron, elders</b> at the <b>tent of meeting</b> into a tent</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Phineas</b> takes a <b>spear</b>, kills both → <b>plague lifted</b></li><li class=\"d1\">given a <b>promised priesthood</b>; <b>Egyptian name</b> (Pinhas) - not a child of Abraham, joined Israel leaving Egypt</li><li class=\"d0\">Started tradition of <b>zealotry</b>: not enough to not sin - if you see sin in God's presence, <b>act</b>, including <b>violence</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Simon the Zealot</b> one of the Twelve; <b>Judas Iscariot</b> likely related to <b>Sicarii</b> = zealot faction, <b>assassins</b> of Romans</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Pharisees</b> wait for God to act when we get it right; <b>zealots</b> take it into <b>their own hands</b> - \"be like the <b>judges</b>, like <b>Phineas</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2299.39,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 10:2-3 - Paul's own zeal; not submitting","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul in this tradition: <b>city to city</b>, orders from the <b>high priest</b>, <b>Stephen stoned</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Zeal, but <b>not according to knowledge</b>\" - \"all the zeal of the world, but I didn't have the knowledge\" - <b>misdirected</b></li><li class=\"d0\">v.3: \"ignorant of <b>God's righteousness</b>, seeking to establish <b>their own</b>, have <b>not submitted</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">God's righteousness = <b>Christ</b> (<b>ch. 3</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">not understanding Jesus as Messiah → seek righteousness <b>another way</b> and <b>miss out</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>submitting</b> echoes \"<b>older shall serve the younger</b>\" - serve the heir</li><li class=\"d1\">they want God to do it <b>their way</b>; \"I'm going to submit myself to that\" - <b>they refuse</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2448.77,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 10:4 - Christ the end of the law","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.4: \"Christ is the <b>end of the law</b> for righteousness to everyone who believes\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>End</b> is not \"it's over, <b>chucked the law in the trash</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Greek <b>*telos</b>* = <b>end in purpose</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Christ is the purpose</b> for which the Torah was given - pointing to him, <b>not to itself</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"The Torah had an <b>aim</b>\" → in Christ God's <b>righteousness</b>, God <b>keeps his promises</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2501.08,"lv":3,"h":"The stumbling block and the Gentiles","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Pharisees' efforts to keep all laws exactly = the <b>stumbling block</b></li><li class=\"d1\">eyes fixed on it - \"<b>pursuing the law as an end in itself</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">they think the Torah kept perfectly is where God keeps promises</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Gentiles</b> \"have it easier\" - no such focus, no big stumbling block (other issues)</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ = where God keeps promises to <b>everyone who is faithful</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"faith\" and \"believe\" = <b>same Greek word</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"all\" = <b>Jews, Gentiles</b>; submit to Christ, the righteousness of God → receive the promises</li><li class=\"d0\">He was the <b>purpose of the law</b> - \"it wasn't an end in itself\"</li></ul>"}]});
